tests/ir/parallel.sky:18:5: note: [GVN-001] `zeros(1000000)` at line 18 reuses the value computed at line 17
   |
18 | b = zeros(1000000)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
17 | a = zeros(1000000)
   |     -------------- computed here first
   = note: applied by the `gvn` pass
   = note: because the earlier `zeros` has the same operands and runs first on every path to this one, so both compute the same value
   = note: because `zeros` can fail, but with the same operands the earlier one has failed already if this one would, and Sky reports the first failure, so this one can never be the failure that is reported

tests/ir/parallel.sky:19:5: note: [FUSE-001] `(a + a) * 2 - a` is rewritten to `fused(a, 2)`
   |
19 | d = (a + b) * 2 - a
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `fuse` pass
   = note: because fused 3 elementwise operations into one kernel; saves 2 temporary arrays of 1000000 elements
   = note: because every intermediate result is used exactly once, by the next operation, and every array operand is a Vector[1000000]
   = note: because 3 of the operations can fail (`add`, `mul`, `sub`); the kernel reports the failure of the first of them in program order that fails at any element, which is the error the separate operations gave

tests/ir/parallel.sky:19:5: note: [PAR-001] this elementwise kernel over 1e6 elements runs on 8 threads
   |
19 | d = (a + b) * 2 - a
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   = note: because every element is independent, so the array is split into contiguous chunks (at most four per thread); the result is bit-identical to the sequential one whatever the split
   = note: because it does not print or call, so no output can change order
   = note: because 3 of its operations can fail (`add`, `mul`, `sub`): if chunks fail, the failing operation that comes first in program order is reported, at its lowest failing element, which is the error a sequential run gives

tests/ir/parallel.sky:19:5: note: [MEM-001] `(a + b) * 2 - a` reuses `a`'s buffer: no allocation for this result
   |
19 | d = (a + b) * 2 - a
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   = note: `a` is not used again after this on any path, and has the shape of the result
   = note: applied by the `lifetimes` analysis of the SIR interpreter

tests/ir/parallel.sky:20:5: note: [PAR-002] these 2 calls are independent (pure): run concurrently on up to 8 threads
   |
20 | x = work(1000)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^
   = note: because `work` has no `print` (directly or in what it calls), and no call uses another's result, so their order cannot show in the output
   = note: because each is expensive enough to start elsewhere: at least 704 estimated operations (the threshold is 400)
   = note: because any of them may fail: every call is awaited and the error of the earliest one in program order is reported, as in a sequential run

tests/ir/parallel.sky:22:5: note: [PAR-003] the call to `noisy` is not run concurrently with the calls after it
   |
22 | p = noisy(1)
   |     ^^^^^^^^
   = note: because `noisy` prints, and output order is behaviour: it stays in program order

